    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the number of ways to have some of the six identical books in the library and the rest checked out such that at least one book is in the library and at least one is checked out. This is equivalent to finding the number of ways to partition 6 books into two subsets with each subset having at least one book. \n\nLet's denote the number of books in the library by \\( k \\). Since each subset must have at least one book, the possible values for \\( k \\) are from 1 to 5 (because if \\( k = 0 \\), there are no books in the library, and if \\( k = 6 \\), there are already enough books in the library to satisfy the condition). For each value of \\( k \\), we need to determine the number of ways to choose \\( k \\) books out of 6 to be in the library.\n\nThe number of ways to choose \\( k \\) books out of 6 is given by the binomial coefficient \\( \\binom{6}{k} \\). Therefore, the total number of ways to have some books in the library and some checked out with at least one book in each category is the sum of the binomial coefficients for \\( k \\) ranging from 1 to 5:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{1} + \\binom{6}{2} + \\binom{6}{3} + \\binom{6}{4} + \\binom{6}{5}\n\\]\n\nWe can calculate each term individually:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{1} = 6\n\\]\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{2} = \\frac{6 \\times 5}{2 \\times 1} = 15\n\\]\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{3} = \\frac{6 \\times 5 \\times 4}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = 20\n\\]\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{4} = \\binom{6}{2} = 15\n\\]\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{5} = \\binom{6}{1} = 6\n\\]\n\nAdding these values together, we get:\n\n\\[\n6 + 15 + 20 + 15 + 6 = 62\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of different ways to have some of the books in the library and the rest checked out, with at least one book in the library and at least one in check-out, is \\(\\boxed{62}\\).",
