      "text": "To determine the number of different ways for some of the books to be in the library and the rest to be checked out, we can use the concept of combinations. We need to choose \\( k \\) books to be in the library, where \\( k \\) can be any integer from 1 to 5 (since at least one book must be in the library). This is equivalent to choosing \\( 6 - k \\) books to be checked out.\n\nThe number of ways to choose \\( k \\) books to be in the library from the 6 books is given by the binomial coefficient \\( \\binom{6}{k} \\). Therefore, the total number of ways to distribute the books such that at least one book is in the library and at least one is checked out is the sum of the binomial coefficients for \\( k \\) from 1 to 5:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{5} \\binom{6}{k}\n\\]\n\nWe can calculate each term in the sum:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{1} = 6\n\\]\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{2} = \\frac{6!}{2!4!} = 15\n\\]\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{3} = \\frac{6!}{3!3!} = 20\n\\]\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{4} = \\frac{6!}{4!2!} = 15\n\\]\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{5} = \\frac{6!}{5!1!} = 6\n\\]\n\nAdding these values together, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{1} + \\binom{6}{2} + \\binom{6}{3} + \\binom{6}{4} + \\binom{6}{5} = 6 + 15 + 20 + 15 + 6 = 62\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of different ways for some of the books to be in the library and the rest to be checked out is \\(\\boxed{62}\\).",
